 "Overcome by sudden faintheartedness while looking at his kinsmen, the warrior's eyes brimmed with tears of despair. The Lord immediately reproached this mental cloudiness as unworthy of noble souls, closing the gates of heaven and bringing disgrace at a decisive moment.": "2.2, 2.3",
 "How can a disciple loose lethal shafts against revered preceptors and grandsires who deserve worship with flowers rather than weapons? Even wandering as a mendicant eating alms would be nobler than feasting on royal luxuries stained with the blood of one's own teachers.": "2.46, 2.47, 2.48",
 "Recognizing that his own judgment is paralyzed by weak-heartedness and moral perplexity over duty, the hero drops his arguments and surrenders completely as a student at the Master's feet, confessing that even unrivaled sovereignty over earth and the celestials could not dry up the grief scorching his senses.": "2.7",
 "Here the true spiritual instruction begins: although you speak words that sound like high philosophy, you are mourning for those who deserve no grief. Men of true knowledge never lament either for bodies from which life has departed or for those in which breath still remains.": "2.11, 2.12",
 "Look at a childhood photograph and compare it with youth and old age: the physical frame has changed completely, yet the conscious person recognizing 'this was I' remains continuous. In the very same way, death is merely the soul stepping into another bodily frame, and the wise are never deluded by it.": "2.13, 2.20",
 "When the five senses touch external objects, they produce fleeting waves of cold and heat, joy and sorrow. Because these experiences have a beginning and an end, they are impermanent by nature; the courageous seeker who endures them with equal vision becomes worthy of liberation.": "2.14, 2.15",
 "Seers of truth who have examined both the transient modifications of matter and the unchanging substratum conclude that what is unreal never acquires true permanence, and what is real never ceases to exist. That imperishable reality pervades the entire cosmos, and no power can ever annihilate it.": "2.16, 2.17",
 "Only the material envelopes are subject to termination, whereas the indwelling owner of the body is immeasurable and deathless. Therefore, anyone who imagines that the inner Self kills another or can be killed by weapons is completely ignorant of spiritual reality.": "2.19, 2.20",
 "Free from the six modifications that afflict all material things, the conscious entity has no birth, growth, decay, or death; it is ancient, unborn, and changeless even when its physical dwelling is struck down. Knowing this immutable nature, how can an enlightened person believe anyone is truly slain?": "2.20, 2.21, 2.22",
 "Just as a person discards threadbare, torn clothes at the end of the day and puts on fresh attire without weeping over the old fabric, the indwelling soul casts aside decrepit physical bodies and enters new ones appropriate for its onward journey.": "2.22",
 "Because the Self has no physical parts orlimbs, sharp swords cannot cut it, blazing fire cannot reduce it to ashes, water cannot dissolve it, and dry winds cannot wither it. Being all-pervading, fixed, unmanifest to the eyes, and beyond mental fabrication, it gives no ground for lamentation.": "2.24, 2.25",
 "Even if we set aside higher metaphysics and adopt the common worldly view that the self dies with the body, grief is still futile. Whatever takes birth must inevitably die, and what dies must take birth again; mourning over an unavoidable law of nature helps no one.": "2.14, 2.20",
 "All creatures emerge from an unmanifest state before birth, appear briefly on the visible stage in the middle, and vanish back into the unmanifest at death—like figures seen in a dream or travelers meeting briefly. Rare is the sage who truly comprehends this wondrous indweller who remains forever invulnerable in every body.": "NONE",
 "Turning from metaphysics to the ethics of one's own station in life, a protector of society must never tremble before a righteous struggle. Such a battle for justice arrives unsought like the wide-open gates of heaven, and only fortunate warriors receive the privilege to fight it.": "2.31, 2.32",
 "If a guardian of dharma turns his back on righteous duty out of personal sentiment, he forfeits both virtue and honor, incurring grave transgression. The world will recount his everlasting infamy, and for a person who has enjoyed high esteem, disgrace is far more painful than physical death.": "2.31, 2.32",
 "The mighty commanders who once held you in high regard will never believe you left the field out of compassion; they will conclude that you fled in terror, and your adversaries will mock your prowess with cruel, unutterable taunts.": "2.34, 2.35",
 "Looked at either way, righteous battle holds no loss: if you fall on the field you ascend to celestial realms, and if you triumph you rule the earth. Therefore stand up with firm resolve, treating pleasure and pain, profit and loss, and victory and defeat as equal so that no sin touches you.": "2.37, 2.38, 2.48",
 "Having imparted the analytical wisdom regarding the Self, the Lord now reveals the discipline of equanimous action by which one breaks the fetters of karma. On this spiritual path no sincere beginning is ever wasted, no partial effort produces harm, and even a small practice rescues the seeker from the terror of samsara.": "2.47, 2.48",
 "A true spiritual aspirant possesses a single, resolute conviction centered on liberation and pleasing the Supreme Lord, whereas those driven by worldly cravings let their intellects splinter into endless branches of competing desires.": "NONE",
 "Unwise people enamored of ornate ritualistic promises declare that nothing exists beyond heavenly pleasures, power, and opulent rebirth. Like a foolish farmer who plants rice only for the straw and throws away the grain, those whose minds are stolen by luxury cannot attain a steady intellect in samadhi.": "NONE",
 "The ritual sections of scripture operate within the three modes of material nature, so the seeker must rise above those three modes, free from dualities and anxiety over gain and safety. When the land is flooded with boundless water on all sides, what need remains for a tiny village well?": "NONE",
 "Human authority extends strictly to the performance of prescribed duty, never to the control or ownership of its eventual results. One must neither work as a mercenary driven by the reward nor slip into lazy passivity out of reluctance to act.": "2.47, 2.48",
 "Just as a traveler who drops his personal claim over imported goods pays no customs tax at the border, the yogi who acts with an even mind in success and failure casts off both merit and demerit right here in this world. That inner poised dedication is the true skill in action.": "2.48, 2.50",
 "When the intellect finally crosses beyond the turbid swamp of delusion, the seeker becomes disenchanted with all worldly rewards heard in the past or promised in the future. Standing unshaken in meditation despite conflicting theological opinions, the wise attain freedom from rebirth and reach the sorrowless abode.": "NONE",
 "Prompted by the question of how an enlightened sage speaks, sits, and moves through daily life, the Lord replies that the primary mark is purely inward: when a person relinquishes all cravings fabricated by the mind and rejoices in the Self alone, he is established in steady wisdom.": "2.55, 2.56",
 "Adversity does not agitate his heart, prosperity awakens no greedy longing, and the three storms of infatuation, fear, and wrath have subsided. Meeting pleasant or unpleasant circumstances without elation or resentment, such a contemplative sage possesses unshakable wisdom.": "2.14, 2.15",
 "Consider how a tortoise instantly pulls its head and four legs beneath its hard shell the moment danger approaches, exposing nothing to harm; in the same manner, the wise practitioner draws every sense organ inward away from tempting sense objects.": "2.58",
 "A sick person or someone observing a strict fast may physically stay away from food and sensory indulgences, yet the subtle mental relish for those objects lingers inside. That deep-seated craving vanishes completely only when one beholds the supreme beauty of the Divine.": "NONE",
 "The senses are so impetuous that they can drag away the mind even of a learned scholar who is actively striving for self-control. Therefore, rather than relying on willpower alone, one must yoke the senses and fix the heart devoutly upon the Supreme Lord.": "2.60, 2.61",
 "Downfall begins quietly in the mind: repeated contemplation of sensory attractions gives birth to attachment, which ripens into craving; when craving is thwarted it erupts as anger, clouding judgment with delusion, erasing memory of one's spiritual identity, ruining the intellect, and plunging the person into destruction.": "2.62, 2.63, 2.64",
 "The solution is not to become an inert stone, but to move through the world with senses governed by a disciplined mind purged of attraction and hatred. Such a person attains pellucid inner tranquility, in which all mental miseries melt away and the intellect stands firm.": "2.48, 2.53, 2.55",
 "Without self-discipline there is neither decisive intellect nor meditative focus, and without peace how can anyone find happiness? If the mind surrenders to even a single roaming sense, its wisdom is capsized just as a fierce wind blows an unanchored ship off course across the waters.": "2.67, 2.68",
 "Ordinary people and the enlightened sage live in opposite worlds of awareness: the spiritual reality in which the self-controlled yogi is wide awake seems like dark night to the ignorant, whereas the feverish pursuit of sense gratification in which the world stays awake is regarded as night by the seeing sage.": "2.69, 2.70",
 "Countless swollen rivers pour their waters into the vast ocean every day, yet the ocean neither overflows its banks nor loses its deep stillness. In the same way, sensory experiences and desires may flow into the sage without disturbing his inner equilibrium, granting him lasting peace.": "2.48, 2.55",
 "Walking through life after shedding all selfish cravings, devoid of the sense of 'mine' and the false ego of 'I', the sage abides in the divine state of Brahman. Having reached this supreme state, one never falls back into delusion and attains final liberation even at the moment of death.": "2.55, 2.72",
